> Actual rolling rows are a table observation

Only the preceding row is needed for the next diagonal recurrence. The ordinary
method therefore uses two rows of size m+1 for a fixed second-input length m,
plus its returned output. It does not allocate the n-by-m table claimed by the
former header. The trace copies each completed real rolling row and records
every actual write in the same producer; it never reruns input prefixes or
recomputes a display table.

The next small example has the contiguous run[2,3] even though a later repeated2
can create a separate suffix of length one. The plotted rows are actual
committed row snapshots, with the zero boundary included.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lcsubstring_rows.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::lcsubstring_trace({1, 2, 3, 2}, {0, 2, 3});
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::print("| actual completed prefix i | actual prefix j | actual suffix length |\n| ---: | ---: | " "---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.rows.size(); ++i) {
        std::vector<double> x, y;
        for (std::size_t j = 0; j < t.rows[i].size(); ++j) {
            x.push_back(j);
            y.push_back(t.rows[i][j]);
            std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", i, j, t.rows[i][j]);
        }
        p.line(x, y, std::format("row{}", i),
               {int(30 + 35 * i), int(70 + 25 * i), int(220 - 25 * i)}, 2);
    }
    p.y_limits(-.1, 3.5).title("Actual completed common-suffix rows").x_label(
        "second-input prefix length j").y_label("actual contiguous suffix length").save_svg("build/lcsubstring_rows.svg");
}
```
