> Verification and references

Native tests exhaust all3969 pairs of binary words through length five and
independently recover every cell’s suffix length by backward contiguous
comparison. They verify every real row, strict record decision, selected
occurrence, work count and quota, retain the original Geeks example, distinguish
substring from subsequence, and cover signed-int extremes, NUL/high bytes, empty
cases and ownership. The ten-report batch passes 95 CTest cases, 30 directly
instrumented ASan/UBSan cases, 121 compiled and executed public examples,
601/601 scoped documentation definitions and 11 representative hovers.
Independent integer, Fraction, graph, string and NumPy references verify 82,729
records, including the complete original seeded LSH study. Frozen comparisons
preserve ordinary legacy results; malformed-input, sentinel-collision, ownership
and explicit-budget corrections are documented above. Thirteen checkout headers
and thirteen installed headers compile independently, and an installed-header
native consumer links and runs. Each complete report program runs against the
checkout library; all four native figures are reviewed and HTML/Markdown exports
preserve computed results and image bytes. Batch receipts:
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-214-223/`.

The common-suffix recurrence is the standard dynamic-programming solution to
longest common substring, with contiguity established by the diagonal extension
and mismatch reset.
[NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures: longest common substring|https://xlinux.nist.gov/dads/HTML/longestCommonSubstring.html]
distinguishes this problem from longest common subsequence; the storage and
exact tie policy here describe this concrete rolling-row producer.
